id=a52sxq_staged_updates_keep
name=Galaxy A52s 5G: Play system updates staged before a ROM update are kept
author=kusti420
description=PackageInstallerService.restoreAndApplyStagedSessionIfNeeded() fails every staged session ("Build fingerprint has changed") when PackageManagerService.isDeviceUpgrading() is true, i.e. when the digest of all partition fingerprints changed. Every K4_UN1CA build changes it (ro.odm.build.fingerprint carries the build stamp, e.g. ...S948BXXS4BZIG.k4202611071244, which PackageManager needs to drop its parsed-package cache and re-dexopt after each flash), so flashing a ROM update while Google Play had a system update staged always threw that update away: on R5CTA0ESFTF 4 trains between 2026-11-01 and 11-03, and the 25-module train (ART, Tethering, Media, ...) never got through. apexd judges the same staged APEX sessions by ro.build.fingerprint only, which is the S26 Ultra base and identical in every K4_UN1CA build, so apexd kept and activated the staged APEXes while PackageManager marked their session failed. Now the staged-session restore treats the boot as an upgrade only when ro.build.fingerprint (Build.FINGERPRINT) differs from the one recorded at the previous boot (persist.sys.k4.base_fp), the same rule apexd uses: a ROM-only update keeps the staged Play system update and it is applied on that boot, a real base change (or the first boot with this patch, nothing recorded yet) fails the sessions as before. Every other isDeviceUpgrading() user (package cache, dexopt, permissions) is untouched.
